import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Files Are Arrows — Arrow-Native Modules","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Files Are Arrows — Arrow-Native Modules","type":"adr","date":"2026-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"Builds on [ADR-0049 — Modules are Evaluated Environments](./0049-modules-are-evaluated-environments.md),\\n[ADR-0052 — Scheme IR Contracts](./0052-scheme-ir-contracts.md),\\nand [ADR-0068 — Predicates as Types](./0068-predicates-as-types.md).","supersedes":["ADR-0056"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0069-arrow-native-modules.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0069-arrow-native-modules.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0069-arrow-native-modules.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0069-files-are-arrows-—-arrow-native-modules" tabindex="-1">ADR-0069: Files Are Arrows — Arrow-Native Modules <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0069-files-are-arrows-—-arrow-native-modules" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0069: Files Are Arrows — Arrow-Native Modules”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0056 proposed a module system for handl based on evaluated environments: a module is the result of evaluating a source file, producing an environment (a binding table mapping names to values). Import binds that environment, field access reads from it, and export controls visibility.</p><p>That model assumes a <strong>statement-based</strong> language: files contain <code>let</code> bindings, <code>export</code> declarations, and <code>import</code> statements that execute sequentially and produce an environment of named values.</p><p>Handl is <strong>arrow-native</strong>. A program is not a sequence of statements — it is a declarative arrow graph. There are no statements, no sequential execution of bindings, no environment produced by running statements in order. The fundamental unit is the arrow, not the binding.</p><p>This creates a mismatch:</p><ol><li><strong>No statement semantics to produce an environment.</strong> ADR-0056&#39;s model requires evaluating statements to build an environment. Arrow-native programs don&#39;t have statements — they have graphs.</li><li><strong>No first-class environment needed.</strong> ADR-0056 requires environments as first-class values so modules can be bound, stored, and field-accessed. Arrow-native programs already have first-class arrows — that&#39;s the entire language.</li><li><strong>Two caches, not one.</strong> ADR-0056 caches evaluated environments (runtime values). Arrow-native programs need both arrow graphs (for execution) and inferred contracts (for verification). The module system must handle both.</li></ol><p>The question: what does &quot;module&quot; mean when there are no statements, only arrow graphs?</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>A file is an arrow.</strong> Modules, imports, and multi-file programs are not a new concept — they are the arrow algebra itself.</p><p>The key refinement since the original proposal: <strong>imports normalize to projections</strong>. This normalization is the semantic abstraction — the meaning of <code>import</code> is <code>project</code>. How that normalization is realized (compile-time desugaring, whole-program flattening, or runtime module loading) is an implementation choice, not a semantic one.</p><h3 id="_1-a-file-is-an-arrow" tabindex="-1">1. A file is an arrow <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-a-file-is-an-arrow" aria-label="Permalink to “1. A file is an arrow”">​</a></h3><p>A <code>.hnl</code> file is an arrow. A single-expression file is that expression&#39;s arrow:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>// collection/sort.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This file IS the arrow <code>loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</code>. Its type is <code>List α ⇝ List α</code>. Running the file runs the arrow. Importing the file imports the arrow.</p><p>A multi-name file is an arrow that produces a <strong>record</strong> of named arrows:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>// collection.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>cons = arr(&quot;list.cons&quot;)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>head = arr(&quot;list.head&quot;)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>sort = loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This is sugar for:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>{ cons: arr(&quot;list.cons&quot;), head: arr(&quot;list.head&quot;), sort: loop(comp(insert, first(id))) }</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The file&#39;s arrow is <code>Unit ⇝ { cons: Arrow, head: Arrow, sort: Arrow }</code>. It produces a record of named arrows. No statements, no environment, no sequential evaluation — just a record literal built from arrow expressions.</p><h3 id="_2-import-normalizes-to-projection" tabindex="-1">2. Import normalizes to projection <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-import-normalizes-to-projection" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Import normalizes to projection”">​</a></h3><p>The <strong>semantic model</strong>: importing a name from a file is projecting a field from the file&#39;s record arrow.</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>import sort from &quot;collection/sort&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Normalizes to:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>sort = project(sort, &lt;collection/sort.hnl&#39;s arrow&gt;)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>You take the file&#39;s arrow (producing a record), project the <code>sort</code> field, and bind it as a name in the current scope. This is ordinary arrow composition — no new concept.</p><p>For a single-expression file (no record), the import binds the file&#39;s arrow directly:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>// sort.hnl is just: loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import sort from &quot;collection/sort&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// normalizes to: sort = &lt;collection/sort.hnl&#39;s arrow&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>The normalization is the abstraction.</strong> The import syntax has exactly one meaning: project a name from a module&#39;s record arrow. Everything else — when projection happens, whether the graph is flattened, whether modules are loaded lazily — is implementation strategy (see §5).</p><h3 id="_3-using-a-name-is-app" tabindex="-1">3. Using a name is App <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-using-a-name-is-app" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Using a name is App”">​</a></h3><p>There are no function calls. Only arrow composition. <code>sort([3, 1, 2])</code> is sugar for:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>const([3, 1, 2]) &gt;&gt;&gt; sort</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A constant producer composed with the sort arrow. In the <code>Scheme</code> IR, this becomes <code>App { callee: &quot;sort&quot;, args: [const([3, 1, 2])] }</code>.</p><p>At runtime: <code>App</code> resolves <code>&quot;sort&quot;</code> to the cached arrow, runs it on the input.</p><p>At compile time: <code>App</code> resolves <code>&quot;sort&quot;</code> to the cached contract, uses it for compositional inference.</p><h3 id="_4-two-caches-graphs-and-contracts" tabindex="-1">4. Two caches: graphs and contracts <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-two-caches-graphs-and-contracts" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Two caches: graphs and contracts”">​</a></h3><p>The module system maintains two caches, both keyed by module path:</p><ul><li><strong>Graph cache</strong>: <code>module path → Scheme</code> — the parsed arrow graph, used for execution</li><li><strong>Contract cache</strong>: <code>module path → Contract</code> — the inferred contract, used for verification</li></ul><p>Both are populated when a module is first imported. Subsequent imports return the cached entries.</p><p>This enables <strong>compositional inference</strong>: each module is inferred once, and its contract is reused by importers. The inference engine does not re-trace imported arrows — it looks up their cached contracts.</p><h3 id="_5-normalization-strategies-—-one-abstraction-many-implementations" tabindex="-1">5. Normalization strategies — one abstraction, many implementations <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-normalization-strategies-—-one-abstraction-many-implementations" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Normalization strategies — one abstraction, many implementations”">​</a></h3><p>The semantic abstraction is fixed: <strong>import = projection</strong>. But the realization of that projection is flexible. Three strategies, all valid implementations of the same abstraction:</p><h4 id="strategy-a-compile-time-desugar-eager-static" tabindex="-1">Strategy A: Compile-time desugar (eager, static) <a class="header-anchor" href="#strategy-a-compile-time-desugar-eager-static" aria-label="Permalink to “Strategy A: Compile-time desugar (eager, static)”">​</a></h4><p>Imports are resolved during parsing/normalization. Each <code>import</code> becomes a <code>project(name, record_arrow)</code> node in the Scheme IR. After normalization, there are no imports — only projections and App references.</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>source: import sort from &quot;collection/sort&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ↓ normalize</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Scheme:  sort = Project { field: &quot;sort&quot;, record: &lt;collection/sort&#39;s Scheme&gt; }</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li><strong>Pros</strong>: No runtime module loading. The IR is self-contained. Inference sees the full graph structure (projections are transparent).</li><li><strong>Cons</strong>: Every import is resolved upfront. Large dependency trees produce large IR. No dynamic loading.</li></ul><h4 id="strategy-b-whole-program-flattening-eager-inlined" tabindex="-1">Strategy B: Whole-program flattening (eager, inlined) <a class="header-anchor" href="#strategy-b-whole-program-flattening-eager-inlined" aria-label="Permalink to “Strategy B: Whole-program flattening (eager, inlined)”">​</a></h4><p>The entire multi-file program normalizes into a single arrow graph. All imports resolve to projections, all projections resolve to inlined subgraphs, all App references resolve to their target arrows. One graph, no module boundaries.</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>main.hnl + collection/sort.hnl + collection/insert.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ↓ flatten</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>single Scheme graph (no Project, no App — all inlined)</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li><strong>Pros</strong>: Maximally simple IR — one graph, no indirection. Inference traces the entire program in one pass. No cache needed.</li><li><strong>Cons</strong>: No separate compilation. Re-flatten on any change. The graph can be very large. Loses module boundaries for debugging.</li></ul><h4 id="strategy-c-runtime-module-loading-lazy-cached" tabindex="-1">Strategy C: Runtime module loading (lazy, cached) <a class="header-anchor" href="#strategy-c-runtime-module-loading-lazy-cached" aria-label="Permalink to “Strategy C: Runtime module loading (lazy, cached)”">​</a></h4><p>Imports are resolved on first access. The module loader parses the file, infers its contract, caches both, and returns the projected arrow. This is the strategy described in §4 (two caches).</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>source: import sort from &quot;collection/sort&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ↓ at first access</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>load collection/sort.hnl → parse → infer → cache</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>return Project { field: &quot;sort&quot;, record: &lt;cached Scheme&gt; }</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li><strong>Pros</strong>: Lazy — only load what&#39;s used. Supports dynamic loading and REPL workflows. Module boundaries preserved for debugging. Cache is shared across imports.</li><li><strong>Cons</strong>: Runtime loading overhead (amortized by cache). Inference must handle App references to cached contracts (cannot always trace through).</li></ul><h4 id="choosing-a-strategy" tabindex="-1">Choosing a strategy <a class="header-anchor" href="#choosing-a-strategy" aria-label="Permalink to “Choosing a strategy”">​</a></h4><p>The three strategies are not mutually exclusive. A compiler might use Strategy A (compile-time desugar) for production builds, Strategy B (whole-program flattening) for whole-program inference, and Strategy C (runtime loading) for REPL/development. The semantic abstraction — import = projection — is the same in all three. Only the timing and representation differ.</p><p>The inference engine is agnostic to the strategy: it sees either <code>Project</code> nodes (A, C) or inlined subgraphs (B), and either <code>App</code> nodes (A, C) or resolved arrows (B). The contract cache (§4) is used in all strategies — even flattening benefits from caching inferred contracts for shared subgraphs.</p><h3 id="_6-no-export-no-visibility-no-environments" tabindex="-1">6. No export, no visibility, no environments <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-no-export-no-visibility-no-environments" aria-label="Permalink to “6. No export, no visibility, no environments”">​</a></h3><p>The arrow-native model does not need:</p><ul><li><strong><code>export</code></strong> — all named arrows in a record are accessible via projection. There is no visibility layer.</li><li><strong>First-class environments</strong> — arrows are already first-class. A record of arrows is just a product type, not a new runtime concept.</li><li><strong>Field access on modules</strong> — import binds names directly into the current scope via projection. No <code>module.field</code> syntax needed.</li></ul><p>If encapsulation or qualified access becomes necessary later, they can be added as sugar without changing the core model.</p><h3 id="_7-multi-file-programs-are-arrow-composition" tabindex="-1">7. Multi-file programs are arrow composition <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-multi-file-programs-are-arrow-composition" aria-label="Permalink to “7. Multi-file programs are arrow composition”">​</a></h3><p>A multi-file program is just arrows composed across files:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>// collection/insert.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>loop(comp(compare, choice(cons, cons)))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// collection/sort.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import insert from &quot;collection/insert&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// main.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import sort from &quot;collection/sort&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>sort([3, 1, 2])</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Normalized (Strategy A — compile-time desugar):</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>// collection/insert.hnl → arrow A_insert: List α ⇝ List α</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// collection/sort.hnl → arrow A_sort: List α ⇝ List α</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>//   where insert = project(insert, A_insert)</span><span>  // A_insert is single-expr, so insert = A_insert</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>//   and the file&#39;s arrow is loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// main.hnl → arrow A_main: Unit ⇝ List Int</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>//   where sort = project(sort, A_sort)</span><span>  // A_sort is single-expr, so sort = A_sort</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>//   and the file&#39;s arrow is const([3, 1, 2]) &gt;&gt;&gt; sort</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Each file is an arrow. Imports normalize to projections. The whole program is a composition of file-arrows. No new concepts.</p><h2 id="behavior-examples" tabindex="-1">Behavior examples <a class="header-anchor" href="#behavior-examples" aria-label="Permalink to “Behavior examples”">​</a></h2><h3 id="example-1-simple-import" tabindex="-1">Example 1: simple import <a class="header-anchor" href="#example-1-simple-import" aria-label="Permalink to “Example 1: simple import”">​</a></h3><p>Filesystem layout:</p><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>project/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  main.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  collection/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    insert.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    sort.hnl</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>collection/insert.hnl</code>:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>loop(comp(compare, choice(cons, cons)))</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>collection/sort.hnl</code>:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>import insert from &quot;collection/insert&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>main.hnl</code>:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>import sort from &quot;collection/sort&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>sort([3, 1, 2])</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Behavior (Strategy C — runtime loading):</p><ol><li><code>main.hnl</code> imports <code>sort</code> from <code>collection/sort</code></li><li><code>collection/sort.hnl</code> imports <code>insert</code> from <code>collection/insert</code></li><li><code>collection/insert.hnl</code> is parsed → arrow graph cached</li><li><code>insert</code> contract inferred (e.g., <code>Sorted(output)</code>) → cached</li><li><code>sort</code> graph parsed (with <code>App { callee: &quot;insert&quot; }</code>) → cached</li><li><code>sort</code> contract inferred (using <code>insert</code>&#39;s cached contract) → cached</li><li><code>main.hnl</code> runs: <code>const([3, 1, 2]) &gt;&gt;&gt; sort</code> → <code>[1, 2, 3]</code></li></ol><p>Under Strategy A (compile-time desugar), steps 2–6 happen at normalization time, producing a self-contained IR with <code>Project</code> and <code>App</code> nodes. Under Strategy B (flattening), steps 2–6 produce a single inlined graph with no <code>Project</code> or <code>App</code> nodes.</p><h3 id="example-2-multi-name-file-record-arrow" tabindex="-1">Example 2: multi-name file (record arrow) <a class="header-anchor" href="#example-2-multi-name-file-record-arrow" aria-label="Permalink to “Example 2: multi-name file (record arrow)”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>// collection.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>cons = arr(&quot;list.cons&quot;)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>head = arr(&quot;list.head&quot;)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>tail = arr(&quot;list.tail&quot;)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>sort = loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This file&#39;s arrow is <code>Unit ⇝ { cons, head, tail, sort }</code>.</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>// main.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import sort, head from &quot;collection&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>head(sort([3, 1, 2]))</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Normalized:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>collection = &lt;collection.hnl&#39;s arrow&gt;  // produces the record</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>sort = project(sort, collection)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>head = project(head, collection)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>const([3, 1, 2]) &gt;&gt;&gt; sort &gt;&gt;&gt; head</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="example-3-contracts-compose-across-files" tabindex="-1">Example 3: contracts compose across files <a class="header-anchor" href="#example-3-contracts-compose-across-files" aria-label="Permalink to “Example 3: contracts compose across files”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>// collection/insert.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>loop(comp(compare, choice(cons, cons)))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// Inferred contract: Sorted(output)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// collection/sort.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import insert from &quot;collection/insert&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>loop(comp(insert, first(id)))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// Inferred contract: Sorted(output) — derived from insert&#39;s cached contract</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// main.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import sort from &quot;collection/sort&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import map from &quot;fn/map&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import filter from &quot;fn/filter&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>comp(comp(map(double), filter(is_even)), sort)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>// Inferred contract: Sorted(output) — derived from sort&#39;s cached contract</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Each file is inferred once. Contracts compose via the cache. No re-inference of imported arrows. This holds under all three strategies — even flattening benefits from caching inferred contracts for shared subgraphs (e.g., when <code>map</code> is imported by multiple files).</p><h3 id="example-4-import-is-idempotent" tabindex="-1">Example 4: import is idempotent <a class="header-anchor" href="#example-4-import-is-idempotent" aria-label="Permalink to “Example 4: import is idempotent”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>import insert from &quot;collection/insert&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>import insert from &quot;collection/insert&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Behavior (all strategies):</p><ul><li>The first import normalizes to <code>project(insert, &lt;collection/insert&#39;s arrow&gt;)</code></li><li>The second import normalizes to the same projection</li><li>Under Strategy C, the cache ensures the file is parsed and inferred once</li><li>Under Strategies A/B, normalization is deterministic — both produce the same projection node, deduplicated by the compiler</li></ul><h2 id="composition-the-only-5-operations" tabindex="-1">Composition: the only 5 operations <a class="header-anchor" href="#composition-the-only-5-operations" aria-label="Permalink to “Composition: the only 5 operations”">​</a></h2><p>There are exactly 5 ways to compose arrows. No more. The module system introduces zero new ones:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>1. Sequential:  a &gt;&gt;&gt; b        (Seq)    — output of a feeds into b</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>2. Parallel:    first(a)       (First)  — a runs on first component, rest passes through</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>3. Conditional: a ||| b        (Choice) — run a OR b based on branch</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>4. Iterative:   loop a         (Loop)   — feedback, repeat until done</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>5. Reference:   app(&quot;sort&quot;)    (App)    — insert a named arrow here</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Files, imports, and modules are sugar over these 5 combinators:</p><ul><li><strong>File</strong> = an arrow (single expression) or <code>Unit ⇝ Record</code> (multi-name)</li><li><strong>Import</strong> = <code>project(name, &lt;file&#39;s arrow&gt;)</code> — field projection, which is <code>First</code> + <code>Arr</code>. This is a <strong>normalization</strong>: import syntax desugars to projection, and the projection is realized by whichever strategy (§5) the implementation chooses.</li><li><strong>Using a name</strong> = <code>App { callee: &quot;name&quot; }</code> — reference composition</li><li><strong>Multi-file program</strong> = composition of file-arrows via projection + App</li></ul><p>No function calls. No method dispatch. No inheritance. No statement ordering. No environments. Just arrows, records, and the 5 combinators.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Multi-file <code>.hnl</code> programs work naturally for arrow-native code.</li><li><strong>No new concepts</strong>: modules are arrows, imports normalize to projections, calls are App. The module system is the arrow algebra.</li><li><strong>Flexible normalization</strong>: the import→projection abstraction admits multiple implementation strategies (compile-time desugar, whole-program flattening, runtime loading). The semantics are fixed; the strategy is chosen per use case (production build, whole-program inference, REPL).</li><li>No first-class environment value needed — arrows are already first-class, records are already product types.</li><li>No <code>export</code> keyword or visibility layer — simpler syntax.</li><li>Compositional inference: each module inferred once, contracts cached and reused. Works under all three strategies.</li><li>The module system and the inference system share the same architecture: a module&#39;s public surface is its arrow + its inferred contract.</li></ul><h3 id="negative-trade-offs" tabindex="-1">Negative / trade-offs <a class="header-anchor" href="#negative-trade-offs" aria-label="Permalink to “Negative / trade-offs”">​</a></h3><ul><li>No encapsulation: all named arrows in a record are accessible via projection. Private helpers cannot be hidden from importers. (Can be addressed later with explicit <code>export</code> as sugar.)</li><li>No qualified namespace access (<code>module.function</code>). All imports are flat name bindings. (Can be added later with <code>import &quot;m&quot; as alias</code> sugar.)</li><li>Two caches (graphs + contracts) instead of one. Slightly more machinery than ADR-0056&#39;s single environment cache. (Under Strategy B — flattening — the cache is optional, but still useful for shared subgraphs.)</li><li>The compiler must resolve <code>App</code> nodes to cached contracts during inference. This is a new responsibility not present in ADR-0056.</li><li>Strategy choice affects tooling: a REPL wants Strategy C, a release build wants Strategy A or B. The toolchain must support multiple normalization passes.</li></ul><h3 id="compatibility" tabindex="-1">Compatibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#compatibility" aria-label="Permalink to “Compatibility”">​</a></h3><p>This design supersedes the module model in ADR-0056 for arrow-native programs. ADR-0056&#39;s environment-based model remains valid for any statement-based evaluation that handl may support in the future, but the arrow-native module system does not depend on it.</p><h2 id="rationale" tabindex="-1">Rationale <a class="header-anchor" href="#rationale" aria-label="Permalink to “Rationale”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0056 assumed a statement-based language where modules are produced by evaluating sequences of bindings. Handl is arrow-native: programs are declarative graphs, not imperative statement sequences.</p><p>The key insight: <strong>a file is an arrow.</strong> A multi-name file is an arrow that produces a record of named arrows. <strong>Import normalizes to projection</strong> on that record. Using an imported name is App composition. The entire module system collapses into the arrow algebra — no new concepts, no new runtime objects, no environments, no field access machinery.</p><p>The second insight: <strong>normalization is an abstraction, not an implementation.</strong> The meaning of <code>import</code> is fixed (project a name from a record arrow), but the realization is flexible. Compile-time desugaring, whole-program flattening, and runtime module loading are three valid strategies for the same abstraction. This lets the toolchain choose the right strategy per context — eager for production, lazy for development, flattened for whole-program inference — without changing the semantics.</p><p>This is simpler than ADR-0056 (fewer concepts, no environment reification, flexible implementation) and integrates naturally with the refinement inference engine (contracts are cached per module, inference is compositional via App resolution, and the normalization strategy is transparent to the inference engine).</p><h2 id="implementation-notes" tabindex="-1">Implementation notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation-notes" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation notes”">​</a></h2><p>The implementation is organized around the normalization abstraction. Each phase adds a strategy or refines an existing one.</p><h3 id="phase-1-—-files-as-arrows" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — files as arrows <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-files-as-arrows" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — files as arrows”">​</a></h3><p>Support single-expression <code>.hnl</code> files. Each file parses to one <code>Scheme</code> arrow graph. Running the file runs the arrow. This is the foundation all strategies build on.</p><h3 id="phase-2-—-records-and-multi-name-files" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — records and multi-name files <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-records-and-multi-name-files" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — records and multi-name files”">​</a></h3><p>Support <code>name = arrow</code> syntax in <code>.hnl</code> files, desugaring to record literals. A multi-name file&#39;s arrow is <code>Unit ⇝ Record</code>.</p><h3 id="phase-3-—-import-normalization-strategy-a-compile-time-desugar" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — import normalization (Strategy A: compile-time desugar) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-import-normalization-strategy-a-compile-time-desugar" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — import normalization (Strategy A: compile-time desugar)”">​</a></h3><p>Add <code>import name from &quot;module&quot;</code> syntax. During normalization, resolve module paths and emit <code>Project { field, record }</code> nodes in the Scheme IR. After normalization, no import syntax remains — only projections and App. This is the default strategy for compiled builds.</p><h3 id="phase-4-—-contract-cache-and-compositional-inference" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — contract cache and compositional inference <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-contract-cache-and-compositional-inference" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — contract cache and compositional inference”">​</a></h3><p>Infer contracts for each imported arrow on first import. Cache them. When the inference engine encounters <code>App { callee: &quot;name&quot; }</code>, look up the cached contract instead of tracing through the graph. This works under both Strategy A (Project nodes reference cached records) and Strategy C (runtime-loaded records).</p><h3 id="phase-5-—-runtime-module-loading-strategy-c" tabindex="-1">Phase 5 — runtime module loading (Strategy C) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-5-—-runtime-module-loading-strategy-c" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 5 — runtime module loading (Strategy C)”">​</a></h3><p>Add a runtime module loader for REPL and development workflows. The loader parses files lazily, infers contracts on first access, and caches both graphs and contracts. Imports normalize to the same <code>Project</code> nodes, but the record arrow is produced by the loader at runtime rather than embedded in the IR at compile time.</p><h3 id="phase-6-—-whole-program-flattening-strategy-b" tabindex="-1">Phase 6 — whole-program flattening (Strategy B) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-6-—-whole-program-flattening-strategy-b" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 6 — whole-program flattening (Strategy B)”">​</a></h3><p>Add a whole-program flattening pass that resolves all <code>Project</code> and <code>App</code> nodes to their target subgraphs, producing a single self-contained Scheme graph with no indirection. Useful for whole-program inference (the engine traces the entire program in one pass) and for producing minimal artifacts. Shared subgraphs are deduplicated; their inferred contracts are reused from the cache.</p><h3 id="phase-7-—-stdlib-as-importable-arrow-files" tabindex="-1">Phase 7 — stdlib as importable arrow files <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-7-—-stdlib-as-importable-arrow-files" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 7 — stdlib as importable arrow files”">​</a></h3><p>Move the stdlib arrow definitions (<code>handl-packages/</code>) to be importable <code>.hnl</code> files. Verify the full cascade works: atoms → insert → sort → user programs, all via imports with compositional inference, under each of the three strategies.</p><h2 id="related-adrs" tabindex="-1">Related ADRs <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-adrs" aria-label="Permalink to “Related ADRs”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./0056-import-export-and-multi-file-programs.html">ADR-0056 — Import / Export and Multi-File Programs as Evaluated Environments</a></li><li><a href="./0049-modules-are-evaluated-environments.html">ADR-0049 — Modules are Evaluated Environments</a></li><li><a href="./0052-scheme-ir-contracts.html">ADR-0052 — Scheme IR Contracts</a></li><li><a href="./0068-predicates-as-types.html">ADR-0068 — Predicates as Types</a></li><li><a href="./0070-refinement-inference-scope.html">ADR-0070 — Refinement Inference Scope</a></li></ul><h2 id="summary" tabindex="-1">Summary <a class="header-anchor" href="#summary" aria-label="Permalink to “Summary”">​</a></h2><p>A file is an arrow. A multi-name file is an arrow that produces a record of named arrows. <strong>Import normalizes to projection</strong> — this is the semantic abstraction. Using an imported name is App composition. The module system is the arrow algebra — no new concepts, no environments, no export visibility.</p><p>The normalization abstraction admits three implementation strategies: compile-time desugar (eager, static IR), whole-program flattening (eager, inlined single graph), and runtime module loading (lazy, cached). All three realize the same semantics. Inference is compositional under all three: each file inferred once, contracts cached and reused via App resolution.</p>`,132)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};